  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:56:41.528Z",
  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nIs an individual required to file an income tax return if they have no income tax due?\n\n### Short Answer\nYes, an individual is still required to file an income tax return even if they have no income tax due. This requirement is established under Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC).\n\n### Accounting Treatment\nFrom an accounting perspective, individuals must maintain accurate records of their income and expenses, even if they do not owe any tax. Filing a return is a formal declaration of income, which is essential for compliance and future reference.\n\n### Tax Treatment\nAccording to Section 51 of the NIRC, every Filipino citizen residing in the Philippines, as well as those residing outside the Philippines on income from sources within the Philippines, are required to file an income tax return. This includes individuals who may not have any tax liability for the year.\n\n### Differences and Reconciliation\nThe distinction lies in the requirement to file a return versus the obligation to pay tax. While individuals with no taxable income may not owe tax, they are still mandated to file a return to report their income status. This ensures compliance with tax regulations and provides a clear record for the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).\n\n### Practical Note\nIndividuals receiving purely compensation income from a single employer, where the tax due equals the tax withheld, may be eligible for substituted filing under Section 51-A of the NIRC. However, this does not exempt them from filing if they have other sources of income or if they choose not to utilize the substituted filing option. It is advisable for individuals to consult with a tax professional to ensure compliance with filing requirements.",
